                    {
                        "sense_id": "45641ea3-14ec-3e9c-9869-94b82a56bad8",
                        "entry_id": "96fa7fbf-293c-3f44-a5db-0ff6efab3053",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To subject to invective; to censure or rail against."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "673ec45d-acc5-48d3-ae94-ee5ec1b0b6ba",
                                "sense_id": "45641ea3-14ec-3e9c-9869-94b82a56bad8",
                                "text": "But, alas! why invect ſo againſt thy tongue? lingua a lingendo, and you know wee uſe alwayes to li[c]ke in, and ſo thou ſhouldeſt keepe in thy poyſon: or a ligando, which is to binde, and ſo thou ſhouldeſt binde up, and not disperſe abroad that ranker in thee."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0351a4d0-86e7-49c6-a8a1-20d2b0e04b30",
                                "sense_id": "45641ea3-14ec-3e9c-9869-94b82a56bad8",
                                "text": "Your pictures far excel you, for they have / All that is good in you, your outward feature, / But your infernal minds they, happy, want. / Beauty, at best, is like a blooming tree, / Fairest in bud, when it bears foulest fruit. / Fool that I am thus to invect against her!"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
